\section{Missing values}{

Unlike Stata, the table functions \code{\link[=s.tabulate]{s.tabulate()}}, \code{\link[=s.tab1]{s.tab1()}}, \code{\link[=s.tab2]{s.tab2()}}
and \code{\link[=s.table]{s.table()}} show missing values as categories by default
(\code{missing = TRUE}), so that the number of missing observations is always
visible. \code{missing = FALSE} excludes them, as Stata does by default. All
other functions exclude missing values, as in Stata.

Missing values are displayed as R prints them, not as Stata's \code{.}, that
is \verb{<NA>} for missing categories, \code{NA} for statistics that cannot be
computed and \code{NA(a)}, \code{NA(b)}, ... for Stata's extended missing values
(haven's tagged missing values). The category columns of the results of
\code{\link[=s.tabulate]{s.tabulate()}} and \code{\link[=s.table]{s.table()}} contain a genuine \code{NA}, so \code{is.na()} and
\code{dplyr::filter()} work as usual.
}

